WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Which of the provided moment diagrams best represents the loading conditions upon the provided beam?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
We can see the beam in question over here.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:25.000
It's a cantilevered beam attached to one side of the wall and has a moment applied at the other side as well as a distributed load in the section between B and A.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
So if you're like me and shear and moment diagrams are not your strong point,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
the first thing to do is go to the reference manual to find some examples.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
I'm not sure if there are any key words that will lead you to this section first,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:50.000
so it will be helpful to remember that the reference manual does contain a section showing shear and moment deflections of various kinds of beams under various types of loading conditions.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
So go there first.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Now let's look at the problem.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:05.000
We can see that the moment applied to the free end of the cantilevered beam is providing a constant amount of bending until an additional force acts on it starting at section B.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:13.000
This section is also the same for all answers, which means we can focus on what's happening in the other two sections instead.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:21.000
The middle section from B to A looks similar to example 19 in the manual on page 244.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:31.000
We can also see that the distributed load is causing the magnitude of the resulting moment to increase non-linearly over the distance from B to A.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
So that would eliminate D from the list of possible answers.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Finally, there is a section from A to the point where the beam is connected to the wall.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:52.000
The first section closest to the anchor point looks like examples 21 and 22 in the manual in the sense that there is no additional force applied to that section,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:59.000
but the moment is still being impacted linearly from the distributed load in the section next to it.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.000
This will instantly narrow down our answer to C.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:13.000
A and D show no additional influence on the moment, and B shows the moment slowly tapering off to the point that there is no additional increase in it.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:22.000
So again, the point of this problem was just to show you a way to use process of elimination and some hints from the manual to get your answer.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Even if statics or structures is not your specialty.
